# Lawha — every setting the server reads, in one file.
#
#   cp lawha.env.example lawha.env
#
# That copy is a REQUIRED first step before `docker compose up`: compose is
# told to load ./lawha.env, and while a missing one is tolerated (see the
# `required: false` note in docker-compose.yml) you will then be running on
# defaults, with a generated admin password you have to read out of the logs.
#
# This file IS committed, so never put a real password in it: every value below
# is a placeholder or a default. Your `lawha.env` is the one that holds
# secrets, and it is gitignored — the root .gitignore carries a bare
# `lawha.env` for exactly that reason. Check that it still does before you fill
# this in; a master password committed to a repository is a master password
# that has to be rotated.
#
# Nothing here is required for the server to boot. Every setting has a default
# in lawha-server/src/config.ts, and this file exists so that you do not have
# to read that file to find out what they are.
#
# ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
# │ READ THIS BEFORE YOU DEBUG ANYTHING FROM THIS FILE                       │
# │                                                                          │
# │ Some settings CANNOT be changed here, and they fail silently rather      │
# │ than loudly. docker-compose.yml lists them in its `environment:` block,  │
# │ and compose gives `environment:` priority over `env_file:` — so the      │
# │ value you write here is read, overridden, and never mentioned again.     │
# │                                                                          │
# │ Currently pinned there: LAWHA_DB_PATH, LAWHA_FILES_DIR and, the one      │
# │ that actually bites, LAWHA_SECURE_COOKIES.                               │
# │                                                                          │
# │ LAWHA_SECURE_COOKIES is pinned to "false", because this deployment is    │
# │ plain http behind a gateway and `Secure` means HTTPS-only. Setting it    │
# │ to true here does nothing — which is a mercy, because true on a plain    │
# │ http origin is the worst failure this project has: the browser accepts   │
# │ the cookie, never stores it, and every visitor is signed out for ever    │
# │ with nothing in any log. If you put TLS back, change it in the compose   │
# │ file — never this line. Each of the three is annotated again below.      │
# └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
#
# WHAT DOES NOT EXIST, deliberately: there is no SMTP block, no mail server, no
# password-reset link and no email column anywhere in the schema. Lawha runs on
# a private network with a named administrator, so the recovery path is the
# admin panel, LAWHA_MASTER_PASSWORD, or the reset-password CLI. Please do not
# add mail "for convenience" — see lawha-server/README.md.


# ─── First boot: how you get in the first time ────────────────────────────────
#
# On a completely empty database the server creates one administrator and stops
# doing so forever after. If BOTH of the next two are set, they are used. If
# either is missing, the account is called `admin` (or LAWHA_ADMIN_USERNAME if
# only that is set) and the server generates a strong password and prints it
# ONCE, in a box, on stdout: `docker compose logs lawha-server`.
#
# The generated route is the safer one — a password that was never written to a
# file cannot be committed by accident. Set these two only if you would rather
# choose.

# 3-32 characters; letters, numbers, dots, dashes, underscores. Also promoted
# to administrator on EVERY boot, which is how you recover from accidentally
# demoting yourself: set it, restart, and the role is back.
# IF WRONG: a name that does not exist yet is simply reported on boot and
# promoted later, when that account is created. Nothing breaks.
#LAWHA_ADMIN_USERNAME=

# Used ONLY at the moment the first account is created. It never changes an
# existing account's password — a value in a file that silently reset someone's
# credentials on every restart would be a back door, not a setting.
# IF WRONG: under 8 characters is refused, loudly, on the boot log, and the
# server seeds a generated password instead rather than leaving you locked out.
#LAWHA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=


# ─── Where the data lives ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# PINNED IN docker-compose.yml — setting them here does nothing under Docker.
# Both point inside the bind mount at ${LAWHA_DATA_DIR:-$HOME/lawha-data}, and
# compose gives `environment:` precedence over `env_file`. They are listed
# because they matter for a bare `yarn --cwd lawha-server start`.

# SQLite, in WAL mode, so expect three files next to it (.db, .db-wal, .db-shm).
# IF WRONG: a path the process cannot write is a hard failure at boot. A path
# that points somewhere new is not an error at all — you get an empty server
# and a first-boot admin banner, which is the usual way people "lose" a board.
LAWHA_DB_PATH=./lawha-data/lawha.db

# Uploaded image blobs and profile pictures. PLAINTEXT on disk since ADR 0012 —
# this line used to say "already client-side encrypted" and that has been false
# since the encryption was removed. Anyone who can read this directory can open
# every picture on every board. LAWHA_BACKUP_RECIPIENT below encrypts the
# archive's copy of them; nothing encrypts these.
# IF WRONG: images stop loading on every board. The scenes are unaffected —
# they live in the database — so it presents as pictures going grey, not as
# lost work.
LAWHA_FILES_DIR=./lawha-data/files


# ─── Automatic backups ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The `lawha-backup` service in docker-compose.yml runs on its own and needs
# nothing set here: it takes a verified backup every 24 hours, keeps the newest
# 14, and mirrors the uploaded blobs. The first one is taken within seconds of
# `docker compose up -d`, not in twenty-four hours' time.
#
# These three ARE readable from this file — unlike LAWHA_DB_PATH above, they
# are deliberately absent from the compose file's `environment:` block so that
# `env_file:` wins. Changing one takes effect on `docker compose up -d`.
#
# WHERE THE FILES GO: ~/lawha-backups on the host. That path is
# LAWHA_BACKUP_DIR, and it belongs in ./.env and not here — see the section at
# the bottom of this file about names compose interpolates. Create it before
# the first start (`mkdir -p ~/lawha-backups`), for the same reason as
# ~/lawha-data: Docker creates a missing bind source as root:root and the
# container runs as the unprivileged `node`. If you forget, the container says
# so in its first two log lines and prints the fix; it does not fail quietly.
#
# HOW A BACKUP IS TAKEN, so that nobody "optimises" it: through SQLite's online
# backup API, in lawha-server/scripts/backup.mjs, which is the only correct way
# to copy this database while the server is writing to it. Never cp, never tar.
# The one-paragraph reason is under "Backup and restore" in README.md and the
# long one is at the top of docker/lawha-backup.sh.

# How often, in whole hours. 0 turns automatic backups off — the container
# stays up, says so once, and reports itself healthy so that "off on purpose"
# never looks like "broken".
# IF WRONG: anything that is not a whole number is refused at start-up with the
# reason on the log, rather than silently becoming a default nobody chose.
#LAWHA_BACKUP_INTERVAL_HOURS=24

# How many backups to keep. Retention verifies every candidate before it counts
# one and refuses to delete a backup it has read in favour of one it has not,
# so an unreadable file in the archive costs you a warning, never a good copy.
# Must be at least 1: 0 would delete the backup the same run just took.
# IF WRONG: too low is the setting that turns "we have a month of history" into
# "we have yesterday" without anybody noticing.
#LAWHA_BACKUP_KEEP=14

# Mirror the uploaded blobs (LAWHA_FILES_DIR) alongside the database, into
# <archive>/files. They are immutable and content-addressed, so a plain copy is
# correct for them — the WAL hazard is specific to SQLite. (This comment used to
# add "and already client-side encrypted". That has been false since ADR 0012:
# the blobs are plaintext, here and in the mirror, unless
# LAWHA_BACKUP_RECIPIENT below is set.) The mirror is append-only and never
# re-copies a file it already has, so it costs one stat per blob per run after
# the first.
# IF WRONG: `false` gives you backups that restore every board perfectly with
# every image permanently grey. The scenes are in the database; the pictures
# are not, and nothing can regenerate them.
#LAWHA_BACKUP_FILES=true

# Mirror this file, ./.env and ./certs into <archive>/config.
#
# Without it the archive is complete and still cannot rebuild the deployment.
# The database and blobs give you everyone's boards; they do not give you the
# certificate every device on the network was told to trust, or the
# administrator password. Restoring without those is a working dataset behind
# a door nobody can open.
#
# IT MAKES THE ARCHIVE SECRET-BEARING, and that is the trade. This file holds
# LAWHA_MASTER_PASSWORD, LAWHA_DB_KEY if you have set one, and certs/ holds the
# CA's private key. The copies are written 0600 and the directory 0700; put the
# archive somewhere that deserves that, and be aware that an off-host mirror
# now carries it too.
#
# LAWHA_DB_KEY IS THE ONE THAT FOLDS BACK ON ITSELF. Turn on the database
# encryption below WITHOUT setting LAWHA_BACKUP_RECIPIENT, and this copy files
# the SQLCipher key in the clear BESIDE THE CIPHERTEXT IT OPENS. Anyone holding
# the archive holds both halves and the encryption has bought them nothing.
# That is not a defect in either setting — it is what "the key lives on the
# same disk" means, applied to the copy of the disk. Two ways out, pick one
# deliberately:
#   * set LAWHA_BACKUP_RECIPIENT too, and the copy becomes config/lawha.env.age
#     (the better answer — the two halves are designed to compose); or
#   * set this to `false` and keep your own copy of lawha.env somewhere the
#     archive is not. You give up a rebuildable archive to do it.
# The container warns about this combination at every start-up, and never
# prints the key.
# IF WRONG: `false` gives you data you can restore and a deployment you cannot
# reach. Note the docker footgun the mounts inherit — a missing bind source
# becomes an empty DIRECTORY, and the script says which host file that means
# rather than copying nothing and reporting success.
#LAWHA_BACKUP_CONFIG=true

# ENCRYPT THE ARCHIVE. An `age` PUBLIC key ("age1" and 58 more characters).
# Empty is the default and leaves every backup in plaintext, which is what this
# deployment is running today.
#
# This is the STRONGER of the two encryption settings in this file, and the
# reason is that only the PUBLIC half ever comes near this machine. The backup
# container can write ciphertext it can never read back — so a server that is
# fully compromised, running as root, cannot decrypt a single backup it has
# ever written. That is a real property, and LAWHA_DB_KEY (further down) does
# not have it.
#
# It is also why `age` is allowed in the image at all, when this same file
# refuses to put rsync or ssh there (see LAWHA_BACKUP_MIRROR_DIR below): those
# need a PRIVATE key inside the container that holds your data. This does not.
#
#   age-keygen -o lawha-backup.key       # NOT on this machine. Keep it safe.
#   grep 'public key' lawha-backup.key   # that line goes here
#
# ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
# │ LOSING THE PRIVATE KEY LOSES EVERY BACKUP. All of them, for ever.        │
# │                                                                          │
# │ There is no escrow, no recovery key and no administrator who can be      │
# │ asked — that is what asymmetric means, and it is inherent rather than a  │
# │ defect. Store the private half somewhere that is not this machine and    │
# │ not this machine's backups, and restore one artefact end to end before   │
# │ you trust the next one.                                                  │
# │                                                                          │
# │ To restore: yarn --cwd lawha-server restore <file>.db.age \              │
# │               --identity ./lawha-backup.key                              │
# └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
#
# APPLY IT WITH ./run.sh — THIS ONE NEEDS A BUILD, NOT A RECREATE.
#
#   ./run.sh
#
# The encryption is done by `age`, a binary that lives in the image's runtime
# stage. It arrives with a BUILD. `docker compose up -d --force-recreate
# lawha-server` — the instruction everywhere else in this file's world — is
# wrong here twice: it does not build, and it
# names lawha-server only, so the scheduler that actually writes the backups
# never even receives the recipient.
#
# It fails CLOSED if you skip the build, which is why it is easy to miss for a
# day: the backup refuses with `could not start "age": spawn age ENOENT`, exits
# 1, writes NOTHING to the archive, and the container goes unhealthy. The
# migration below prints `age: command not found` and leaves the blobs
# plaintext. Nothing is deleted and no plaintext is ever passed off as
# ciphertext — what you lose is backup continuity, until somebody looks.
#
# AFTER SETTING THIS, RUN THE BLOB MIGRATION ONCE. It is required, not tidy-up:
#
#   docker compose exec lawha-backup /opt/lawha/lawha-backup.sh --encrypt-existing-blobs
#
# The blob mirror decides "already copied" by filename and never revisits one.
# Without the migration, every image already in the archive stays plaintext for
# ever AND the next cycle writes an encrypted twin beside it rather than
# replacing it. Both then persist. The container warns on every start until it
# is done; the migration is idempotent and safe to repeat.
#
# What gets encrypted: the database artefact (AFTER integrity_check and the row
# counts — a corrupt file encrypted promptly is still corrupt), the config
# mirror above, every new blob, and the quarantined .rejected files.
# IF WRONG: a value that is not a well-formed age recipient is refused at BOOT,
# by name, rather than six hours later in a backup log nobody reads. A key you
# no longer have the private half of is the failure this setting really has,
# and nothing detects it except trying a restore.
#LAWHA_BACKUP_RECIPIENT=age1...

# A second, OFF-HOST copy of the whole archive.
#
# EMPTY BY DEFAULT because it names a path that has to exist first, and this is
# the single biggest hole left in the policy: the default archive
# (~/lawha-backups) sits on the same disk as the data (~/lawha-data), so one
# disk failure takes both.
#
# It is a MOUNTED PATH, not an rsync or ssh target, and that is deliberate.
# The runtime image contains neither binary, and adding them would also mean
# putting a private key inside the container whose entire job is to hold your
# data. Mount the destination on the HOST — second disk, NAS over NFS/SMB,
# sshfs — expose it to lawha-backup in docker-compose.yml, and name it here.
#
# It runs LAST, after backup.mjs has integrity-checked and row-counted the
# artefact: a corrupt file copied promptly is still corrupt.
# IF WRONG: a path that is set but not mounted is refused at START-UP, loudly,
# rather than failing every interval into a log nobody reads.
#LAWHA_BACKUP_MIRROR_DIR=/offsite

# Not a Lawha setting, but it lands here: TZ sets the container's clock, and
# `backup.mjs` stamps filenames — lawha-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS.db — in local time.
# Unset, the container is UTC, so automatic backups and a manual one taken on
# the host per the README carry timestamps from different zones. They still
# sort and prune sanely; the names just do not line up with each other. Set
# this to your host's zone if that bothers you.
#TZ=Europe/Istanbul


# ─── Encrypting the live database ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# READ WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO BEFORE YOU READ WHAT IT DOES.
#
# LAWHA_DB_KEY DOES NOT PROTECT A STOLEN MACHINE. The key sits in this file, on
# the same disk as lawha.db, because the server has to restart unattended and
# cannot prompt anybody for a passphrase. Whoever takes the box has both.
#
# What it protects is a copied FILE: a stray lawha.db on a share, a
# decommissioned drive, a backup restored to the wrong place, a tar handed to
# somebody who needed the boards and not the credentials. That is a smaller
# claim than "encryption at rest" usually implies and it is the true one. If
# what you want is "somebody takes the laptop and gets nothing", the setting
# that delivers that is LAWHA_BACKUP_RECIPIENT above, and only for the archive.
#
# The full reasoning, the measurements and the migration are in
# docs/adr/0020-encryption-at-rest.md and docs/configuration.md.

# SQLCipher on lawha.db itself, via better-sqlite3-multiple-ciphers. Empty is
# the default and means a plain SQLite file — which is what this deployment is
# running today.
#
# At least 16 characters. GENERATE IT (`./run.sh secret`), do not type it: the
# passphrase is stretched with PBKDF2 and is the whole of the strength. The
# floor exists to catch `changeme`, not to certify anything.
#
# SETTING THIS ON AN EXISTING DEPLOYMENT DOES NOT ENCRYPT IT. The server will
# refuse to boot against a plaintext file with a key set, and say so. The
# conversion is one command, run with the stack stopped, and it never deletes
# anything — the plaintext original is kept as lawha.db.pre-encryption:
#
#   ./run.sh stop
#   LAWHA_DB_KEY='<this value>' LAWHA_DB_PATH=~/lawha-data/lawha.db \
#     corepack yarn --cwd lawha-server encrypt-db
#
# LAWHA_DB_PATH must be passed explicitly — the default above is right for a
# checkout and does not exist under Docker. Expect lawha.db to get SMALLER
# (freelist pages are dropped; that is compaction, not loss). Keep
# lawha.db.pre-encryption until the server has started, you have seen your
# boards, AND you have proven a backup.
#
# THIS FILE REACHES THE CONTAINERS ONLY. The scheduled backup and the /admin
# download read LAWHA_DB_KEY from here and keep working. A backup or restore
# you run BY HAND on the host does not see it and refuses by name — export it
# for those. That is the most reachable mistake this setting creates.
#
# AND THIS FILE IS COPIED INTO THE ARCHIVE. With LAWHA_BACKUP_CONFIG=true (the
# default) and no LAWHA_BACKUP_RECIPIENT, config/lawha.env in every backup
# carries this key in the clear, beside the ciphertext it opens — so the
# archive gives up both halves at once and this setting protects nothing there.
# Either set a recipient as well, or set LAWHA_BACKUP_CONFIG=false and keep
# your own copy of this file elsewhere. Read the note beside
# LAWHA_BACKUP_CONFIG above before you decide; the container warns at start-up
# in exactly this combination.
#
# WHAT IT COSTS: opening the database goes from 0.08 ms to about 83 ms (PBKDF2,
# once per process at boot, not per request), reads are roughly 15x slower, and
# /admin's "Back up now" stops yielding while it copies.
#
# LOSING THIS VALUE LOSES EVERY BOARD. There is no recovery path and no second
# tool that undoes the encryption.
# IF WRONG: the server REFUSES TO START and names which of the four mistakes it
# is — wrong key, no key against an encrypted file, a key against a plaintext
# one, or a damaged file. That refusal is the feature, not a rough edge: a boot
# that continued would find an empty database, seed a first-boot administrator
# and print a new password, which looks exactly like your data having been
# wiped while it sits intact on disk. Your data is fine. Fix the key. Do not
# restore over it.
#LAWHA_DB_KEY=


# ─── Listening ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The port THIS PROCESS listens on, which is not the port you type into a
# browser. Behind the supplied compose file nothing needs to change here: 3002
# is reached by nginx over the internal docker network and is never published.
#
# The published ports are 443 and 80, both mapped straight through to nginx in
# docker-compose.yml, so the deployment answers at:
#
#     https://lawha.local              the address everyone types
#     https://<this-box's-lan-ip>      the same app, for a client with no mDNS
#
# Port 80 is bound too, and serves NO application: /healthz, /lawha-ca.pem, and
# a redirect to https for everything else. https, not http, everywhere else:
# window.crypto.subtle is absent outside a secure context and Lawha cannot mint
# or open a board without it.
# IF WRONG: a port already in use fails at boot.
LAWHA_PORT=3002

# 0.0.0.0 listens on every interface. 127.0.0.1 makes the server unreachable
# from anywhere but the machine it runs on.
# IF WRONG: 127.0.0.1 inside a container means the container is unreachable
# from nginx, and the stack comes up "healthy" but every request times out.
LAWHA_HOST=0.0.0.0


# ─── Who may connect ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# true: an unauthenticated connection is refused. false: every connection is
# handed one shared `anonymous` identity instead.
# IF WRONG: `false` on anything reachable means the whole LAN shares one
# account and can co-own each other's boards. Note that people holding a share
# link are NOT affected by this — they get a separate, narrower, view-only
# principal scoped to one board. Never widen this to serve them.
LAWHA_REQUIRE_AUTH=true

# false means accounts are created by an administrator only; the sign-in screen
# then hides "Create one" rather than offering a form that will 403.
# IF WRONG: `true` on a server anyone can reach lets strangers register.
LAWHA_ALLOW_OPEN_REGISTRATION=true

# >>> PINNED IN docker-compose.yml. EDITING THIS LINE DOES NOTHING UNDER
# >>> DOCKER. `environment:` outranks `env_file:`, and the compose file sets
# >>> LAWHA_SECURE_COOKIES: "false", because since ADR 0018 nothing in this
# >>> stack terminates TLS — a gateway on the network owns port 80 and Lawha
# >>> publishes one plain-http port. To change it you change
# >>> docker-compose.yml. This line only applies to a bare
# >>> `yarn --cwd lawha-server start`.
#
# This comment said "true" for two days, giving the opposite of both the
# value and the reason. Compose is the source of truth; read it, not this.
#
# Adds `Secure` to the session cookie, so browsers only send it over HTTPS.
# This and TLS are ONE change wearing two hats and must move together.
# IF WRONG: `true` without TLS breaks sign-in outright — the browser never
# sends the cookie back and every request looks signed out, which is the exact
# symptom that sends people to edit this file. `false` with TLS leaves the
# session cookie usable on a plain-http origin, which is the only thing the
# flag exists to prevent.
LAWHA_SECURE_COOKIES=false

# How long a session lasts, in days. 0 — the default, and what is set below —
# means it never expires: the row is written with a year-9999 sentinel and the
# hourly sweep is told in SQL to leave it alone. Any positive number is a real
# expiry, rolling: it is refreshed in its last day, so an active session does
# not end mid-drawing.
#
# "Never" is a promise this server can keep and a browser cannot. Chrome and
# Safari both cap a cookie at roughly 400 days whatever Max-Age is sent, so the
# cookie is issued for 400 days and RE-ISSUED on the visits in between. Someone
# who keeps using Lawha never signs in again; someone who does not open it for
# more than a year signs in once more. There is no setting that changes this and
# nothing is broken when it happens.
#
# It is not the thing keeping a lost laptop out, and was never meant to be:
# logout deletes the session, changing a password deletes every OTHER session on
# every other device, and the admin panel can drop a user's sessions outright.
# All three are immediate and none of them depend on this number.
#
# IF WRONG: 0 is "never", not "expire immediately" — a zero-length session would
# sign everybody out on the request after they signed in, and that is not what
# this does. A negative value is refused at boot rather than minting sessions
# that are already in the past. Too short a positive value and a whole team
# re-authenticates constantly.
#
# Changing this reaches sessions that ALREADY EXIST, the next time each one is
# used: turning expiry off makes live sessions permanent, and turning it back on
# brings permanent ones back under the new lifetime. Neither waits for a
# sign-out.
LAWHA_SESSION_TTL_DAYS=0


# ─── Recovery ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# A password that signs in as ANY account. This is the "call the administrator"
# path, in place of the mail flow that does not exist. Sessions opened with it
# are flagged on the session row, printed to the server log, and stated plainly
# in that session's UI, so acting as someone else is never silent. It is tried
# only after the account's own password has already failed, and costs the same
# argon2 verification.
# Unset disables the mechanism entirely, which is the default.
# IF WRONG: this is a plaintext secret in your environment — treat it as
# equivalent to the database itself. A weak one is a master key to every
# account on the server.
#LAWHA_MASTER_PASSWORD=


# ─── Rate limiting ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Loose per IP on purpose: a whole office can sit behind one NAT address, so an
# address is barely an identity here. All of it is in memory, so restarting the
# server clears every bucket.
#
# ALL THREE ACCEPT 0, WHICH MEANS OFF — not "allow nothing", which is the other
# reading and would be a lockout of everybody. 0 is this project's convention
# for a disabled setting (see LAWHA_BACKUP_INTERVAL_HOURS, LAWHA_SESSION_TTL_DAYS).
#
# Turning sign-in limits off is reasonable on a LAN you own and reckless on
# anything `./run.sh public` can reach. The tunnel puts this form on the open
# internet; `run.sh public` warns when the limits are off, on every start.

# Sign-ups per address per hour. 0 = unlimited.
# IF WRONG: too low and the fifth colleague to sign up on their first morning
# is locked out. This exact limit, at its old value of 5, wasted a debugging
# session by looking like a broken registration form.
LAWHA_REGISTER_LIMIT_PER_IP=40

# Sign-in attempts per address per 15 minutes. 0 = unlimited.
# IF WRONG: too low locks out a shared office; too high is mostly harmless,
# because the per-username limit is the one doing the work.
LAWHA_LOGIN_LIMIT_PER_IP=60

# Failed sign-ins per USERNAME per 15 minutes, and the threshold for the
# escalating delay that goes with it — one number, because they are one intent.
# 0 = unlimited, and no delay.
#
# THIS IS THE LIMIT THAT MATTERS. An attacker can spread guesses against one
# account across every address they have; they cannot spread them across the
# username they are trying to break into. The per-IP number above is nearly
# decorative next to this one.
#
# It was hard-coded at 5 and this file used to say it was not configurable,
# which was true and meant the only exit from a lockout was restarting the
# server — the buckets are in memory. That is fine for a stranger guessing and
# infuriating for an administrator testing their own deployment.
#
# IF WRONG: 0 on anything internet-reachable leaves nothing between an attacker
# and unlimited guesses at a named account. Too low and five typos lock a real
# person out of their own work for fifteen minutes.
LAWHA_LOGIN_LIMIT_PER_USERNAME=5


# ─── Origins, proxies and the two ways in ─────────────────────────────────────
#
# The supplied stack is single-origin: nginx serves the app and proxies /api
# and /socket.io to this process, so the browser sees one origin and this
# section can stay commented out. It stops being optional the moment anything
# in front of nginx rewrites the Host header.

# Comma-separated list of origins allowed to make non-GET requests, AND the
# origins allowed to open a relay socket. One value, two consumers —
# src/http/middleware/csrf.ts and src/socket/index.ts read the same list and
# apply the SAME rule, so a name that can save can also collaborate.
#
# Not "the socket.io CORS allowlist", which is what this line used to say and
# what the code used to rely on. socket.io's `cors` option is a grant, not a
# guard: for a disallowed origin it omits a header and calls next() without an
# error, so the handshake completes anyway — measured, 200 with a usable sid on
# polling and 101 on a raw websocket upgrade. The refusal is `allowRequest` in
# src/socket/index.ts, which runs on the first request of both transports.
# `cors` is still there, and still only grants: a genuine split-origin browser
# needs that header to READ the polling handshake.
#
# Each entry is an EXACT origin: scheme, host and port, no path, no trailing
# slash (a trailing slash is stripped for you; nothing else is). The port is
# part of it. `https://lawha.local` and `https://lawha.local:9002` are two
# different origins and neither matches `http://lawha.local`.
#
# If you set this at all, set every address anyone reaches the box on:
#
#     LAWHA_ORIGIN=https://lawha.local,https://192.168.1.50
#
# WHEN YOU NEED IT: when the Host header reaching this process is not the host
# the browser typed. nginx forwards `$http_host` — the browser's Host, port
# included — so with this stack in front, every address matches without any
# entry here. Something that rewrites Host on the way through does not, and
# that is what this list is for.
#
# IF WRONG, and this is why it is worth getting right first time, it presents
# as TWO unrelated bugs at once:
#   - every write 403s with "Request origin not allowed" while every read keeps
#     working — boards open, nothing saves, renames and deletes fail. That
#     reads as a broken product, not a config error.
#   - the relay refuses the handshake, so live collaboration, remote cursors
#     and presence go quiet — with no error anywhere the user can see, because
#     the reason is written to the server's connection_error context and never
#     sent to the client.
# Both from one missing or misspelled entry.
# Behind the gateway this is usually EMPTY, and empty is the right answer.
#
# The CSRF check accepts a request whose Origin host equals the Host header, and
# the gateway passes Host through — so http://lawha.local and
# http://localhost:9002 both satisfy it with nothing listed here.
#
# Fill it in only if the gateway REWRITES Host rather than forwarding it. The
# symptom is specific and worth recognising: every read works and every write
# is refused with "Request origin not allowed", so boards open and nothing can
# be saved, renamed, shared or deleted. That reads as a broken product rather
# than as a missing setting.
#
# Scheme included, no trailing slash, comma-separated. http, not https.
#LAWHA_ORIGIN=http://lawha.local,http://localhost:9002

# Comma-separated list of this deployment's LAN addresses, in preference
# order — the first is primary, the rest are fallbacks (a hostname that needs
# mDNS, then the raw IP that always works). Read by the share panel so a link
# handed to someone on the same network goes over the LAN instead of carrying
# whichever address the sharer's own browser happened to be on.
#
# THIS IS NOT LAWHA_ORIGIN, even though the two sit next to each other and
# both hold origins. LAWHA_ORIGIN is compared against the browser's `Origin`
# header for CSRF and the socket handshake; LAWHA_LAN_ORIGINS is never
# compared against anything — it is only ever handed to a client to render as
# a link. Setting one does not set the other, and merging them would mean an
# address meant only for display starts being trusted for write requests.
#
# Each entry is an EXACT origin, same rule as LAWHA_ORIGIN: scheme, host and
# port, no path, no trailing slash (stripped for you; nothing else is).
#
# The raw IP is worth including even though it is uglier than the mDNS name:
# `lawha.local` needs mDNS, which not every device or network resolves, and
# the IP is the fallback that always works. It also breaks the moment DHCP
# moves this box to a different address. Unlike a setting nobody remembers to
# check, THAT failure is loud and local: the address is edited by hand right
# here, so a moved box is a five-second edit to this file, not a silent wrong
# link handed to someone.
#
# SET THIS. It is commented out here like everything else in this file, and it
# is the one setting whose absence is invisible from every screen.
#
# The share panel builds one row per address this deployment publishes.
# LAWHA_PUBLIC_SHARE_ORIGIN is derived from NGROK_DOMAIN automatically, in
# docker-compose.yml. LAWHA_LAN_ORIGINS is the only one that needs a hand edit,
# and nothing prompts for it — run.sh checks four other settings and never
# mentions this one.
#
# So leave it unset with a tunnel configured and the panel has exactly ONE
# route to offer, and offers it: a colleague at the next desk copying a board
# link gets the NGROK URL, and the same-origin link they used to get is
# REPLACED rather than added to. Nothing on screen says a LAN route is missing,
# because the panel cannot tell "none published" from "there isn't one".
#
# THE PROBLEM IS THAT THE NGROK LINK WORKS. Intra-office board sessions get
# routed out through a third party that terminates TLS, out to the internet and
# back, burning the free plan's 1 GB and 20,000 requests a month — and nobody
# reports it, because from the inside it just looks like Lawha being slow.
#
# The same rows are what /admin offers when it hands you a password reset link,
# where the recipient is by definition somebody who cannot sign in and cannot
# tell you the link you sent does not resolve for them.
#
# Scheme included, no trailing slash, comma-separated.
#LAWHA_LAN_ORIGINS=http://lawha.local,http://192.168.1.50:9002

# How many reverse-proxy hops in front of this process to trust, for Express'
# `trust proxy`. This decides which X-Forwarded-For entry becomes the client
# address the per-IP rate limits key on.
#
# 1, the default, is right for the supplied stack: nginx is the only thing that
# can reach this process, and it APPENDS its own view of the peer to
# X-Forwarded-For, so the last entry is observed rather than asserted. Never
# set this to a value that makes an entry the CLIENT wrote the key — that is
# what the old `trust proxy: true` did, and it let any visitor pick a fresh
# rate-limit bucket per request with one header.
#
# 2 if a LAN gateway sits in front of nginx AND forwards X-Forwarded-For.
# 0 if nothing proxies this at all.
# IF WRONG: too low and everyone behind the gateway shares one bucket, so one
# person fumbling a password locks the whole LAN out of sign-in for fifteen
# minutes. Too high and the limits stop limiting anything.
# TWO, for this deployment, and the default of 1 is wrong here.
#
# There are two proxies in front of lawha-server now: the gateway that owns the
# name, and lawha-app (nginx) inside the stack. Counting only one means the
# address every request appears to come from is nginx's, so the whole network
# shares a single rate-limit bucket and one person fumbling a password locks
# everyone out of sign-in for fifteen minutes.
LAWHA_TRUST_PROXY_HOPS=2

# ─── LAWHA_* names that do NOT belong in this file ────────────────────────────
#
# Written down here because this is the file people open when they want to
# change an address, and none of these can be changed from it — silently,
# which is the same failure the box at the top of this file describes, wearing
# the other hat. `env_file:` reaches the CONTAINER's environment. Compose's own
# `${...}` interpolation reads the SHELL and ./.env and nothing else, so a name
# that docker-compose.yml interpolates is invisible here no matter what you put
# beside it.
#
#
#   LAWHA_PUBLIC_ORIGIN, LAWHA_HTTPS_SUFFIX and LAWHA_CANONICAL_ORIGIN were
#                         all described here until 2026-08-14, at length. They
#                         went with the TLS configuration in ADR 0018 and now
#                         appear NOWHERE in this repository: not in
#                         docker-compose.yml, not in docker/nginx.conf, not in
#                         lawha-server. Setting any of them does nothing at
#                         all. They are named here rather than deleted in
#                         silence, because a deployment that copied them
#                         forward deserves to be told they stopped meaning
#                         anything. HTTPS in the stack is LAWHA_TLS in ./.env
#                         now — see ADR 0022.
#
#   LAWHA_PUBLIC_SHARE_ORIGIN  The origin this deployment is reachable at from
#                         OUTSIDE the network. One value, and it already
#                         exists: `NGROK_DOMAIN` in ./.env, validated by
#                         run.sh (must start with https://) and interpolated
#                         into the ngrok service's `--url` flag in
#                         docker-compose.yml. That same file ALSO feeds it to
#                         lawha-server's `environment:` block, as
#                         `LAWHA_PUBLIC_SHARE_ORIGIN: ${NGROK_DOMAIN:-}`, so
#                         the tunnel and the server agree by construction
#                         instead of by two people remembering to keep two
#                         files in sync. Writing LAWHA_PUBLIC_SHARE_ORIGIN
#                         HERE does nothing — `environment:` outranks
#                         `env_file:`, same trap as LAWHA_SECURE_COOKIES
#                         above — and a second, hand-typed copy that drifts
#                         from NGROK_DOMAIN is a dead link handed to someone
#                         outside the network, which is the exact failure
#                         this whole setting exists to prevent. Change the
#                         address in ONE place: NGROK_DOMAIN in ./.env.
#
#
#
#   LAWHA_BACKUP_DIR      The host directory the `lawha-backup` service writes
#                         its archive to, bind-mounted at /backups. Defaults to
#                         ~/lawha-backups, which is also the path the manual
#                         command in README.md uses, so the two share one
#                         archive and one retention policy. Absolute path: a
#                         relative one resolves against docker-compose.yml and
#                         puts your only copy of the database inside the
#                         repository. Do not point it inside ~/lawha-data — a
#                         backup that lives in the directory it protects is not
#                         a backup. The five LAWHA_BACKUP_* settings above are
#                         a different matter and DO belong in this file.
#
# LAWHA_DATA_DIR is another; see the volume comment in docker-compose.yml. Put
# all of them in ./.env (gitignored) or export them.


# ─── Single-process deployment ────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Serve the built frontend from this process instead of from nginx. This is
# what `yarn lan` sets. Reachable ONLY from the machine it runs on: Lawha needs
# a secure context to mint a board key, and http://<lan-ip> is not one.
# IF WRONG: pointing at a directory with no index.html makes every client route
# 404. Pointing at a stale build serves yesterday's app with today's API.
#LAWHA_STATIC_DIR=./excalidraw-app/build
